PR #2978  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Hardwiring mstatus/mstatush control bits to read-only despite the associated feature being present silently disables M-mode's ability to enforce privilege boundaries, trap lower-mode operations, and configure memory-access policy.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:13-19  (top-level defines)
      The kernel defines only 7 mstatus/sstatus bit masks (SR_SIE, SR_MIE, SR_SPIE, SR_MPIE, SR_SPP, SR_MPP, SR_SUM). Nine additional privileged control bits specified by the RISC-V ISA — MPRV, MXR, MBE, SBE, UBE, TVM, TW, TSR — are completely absent: no symbolic constant, no runtime check, no initialization. If a hardware implementation (or M-mode firmware on an S-mode kernel) hardwires any of these bits to read-only 0 while providing the corresponding feature, the kernel has zero visibility into the mismatch. In CONFIG_RISCV_M_MODE kernels, the kernel directly writes CSR_MSTATUS but still never touches these bits, meaning the kernel itself could silently fail to configure isolation controls it assumes are active.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:487-532  (top-level defines)
      The M-mode/S-mode CSR aliasing layer maps SR_IE→SR_MIE/SR_SIE, SR_PIE→SR_MPIE/SR_SPIE, SR_PP→SR_MPP/SR_SPP, but provides NO corresponding alias for MPRV, MXR, MBE, SBE, UBE, TVM, TW, or TSR. This means the kernel's generic CSR_STATUS abstraction cannot express these bits at all — code that writes CSR_STATUS has no portable way to manipulate the missing bits across M-mode and S-mode configurations. The aliasing infrastructure structurally embeds the assumption that only IE/PIE/PP/SUM matter.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:144-153  (top-level defines)
      HSTATUS_VTSR (0x00400000), HSTATUS_VTW (0x00200000), HSTATUS_VTVM (0x00100000), and HSTATUS_VSBE (0x00000020) are defined for KVM guest execution — confirming these control bits exist in the ISA and the kernel understands their semantics when virtualizing. However, no bare-metal mstatus/sstatus constants for TSR (bit 22), TW (bit 21), TVM (bit 20), or SBE (bit 36, on RV64) are defined. This asymmetry shows the kernel recognises these bits are architecturally meaningful yet treats them as exclusively a hypervisor concern, never checking or initializing them for native execution — creating a blind spot on hardware that hardwires them.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:415-415  (top-level defines)
      CSR_MSTATUS (0x300) is correctly defined. The absence of CSR_MSTATUSH (0x310) is consistent with the broader pattern — on RV32, mstatus is split across two 32-bit CSRs (0x300 and 0x310), and the kernel has no representation for the high half. Since the missing mstatus bits (MPRV, MXR, MBE, SBE, UBE) reside in the lower 32 bits of mstatus on RV64 and in the same 32-bit space as the well-supported bits, this definition gap is less severe than the others. However, on RV32, the high-half mstatus fields (SBE=bit 4 in mstatush, MBE=bit 5) would be inaccessible without a CSR_MSTATUSH constant.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/irqflags.h:19-22  arch_local_irq_enable
      csr_set(CSR_STATUS, SR_IE) correctly writes the IE bit through the aliased CSR_STATUS abstraction (which resolves to MIE/SIE depending on CONFIG_RISCV_M_MODE). This is a well-trodden, correct path. The concern is not that this is wrong, but that it demonstrates the kernel infrastructure handles IE properly while providing zero equivalent tooling for TVM, TSR, TW — meaning if those bits were hardwired to 0 on a given implementation, the kernel would neither notice nor compensate.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/irqflags.h:25-28  arch_local_irq_disable
      csr_clear(CSR_STATUS, SR_IE) is the correct counterpart to arch_local_irq_enable. Same assessment: the IRQ enable/disable path is correct and well-exercised, but its existence as the sole mstatus manipulation in kernel infrastructure highlights that no comparable macros exist for the missing control bits. The security concern is systemic — the kernel has thorough infrastructure for IE but none for TVM/TSR/TW/MPRV/MXR.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/irqflags.h:31-34  arch_local_irq_save
      csr_read_clear(CSR_STATUS, SR_IE) performs an atomic RMW on the IE bit — correct and safe. Once again, this is evidence that the IE bits (SIE/MIE) are the primary mstatus bits actively managed by kernel IRQ infrastructure. No atomic RMW operations exist for TVM, TSR, TW, MPRV, or MXR anywhere in the kernel.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/uaccess.h:59-60  __enable_user_access
      csrs sstatus, SR_SUM directly sets SUM in sstatus. This works on S-mode kernels because SR_SUM is writable via sstatus (a restricted view of mstatus). However, there is no guard checking whether SUM is actually writable — if a hardware implementation hardwires SUM to read-only 0 despite having S-mode and U-mode, this csrs instruction would silently become a no-op. The kernel would then attempt user memory access with SUM=0, causing spurious page faults caught by fixup_exception(), resulting in -EFAULT to userspace rather than a privilege escalation. The silent-hardwiring of SUM would be a functional failure (userspace syscalls fail) rather than a security vulnerability in the traditional sense, but the lack of any runtime writability check is the pattern the spec flags.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/uaccess.h:61-62  __disable_user_access
      csrc sstatus, SR_SUM correctly clears SUM. Paired with __enable_user_access — no additional concerns beyond those noted above.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/process.c:144-147  start_thread
      Initializes new user thread with regs->status = SR_PIE (SPIE/MPIE=1). This sets the Previous-IE bit so that on first sret/mret, interrupts will be enabled in user mode. Critically, this initializes status to exactly SR_PIE and nothing else — leaving 49+ other mstatus bits (including MPRV, MXR, TVM, TW, TSR, SUM, endianness bits) at whatever value was left in pt_regs->status from the kernel path that created the thread. The kernel does not sanitize or validate the full status register value before user entry. If M-mode firmware or a prior kernel context left residual bits in status, a new user thread could inherit unexpected machine state.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/process.c:253-257  copy_thread
      Kernel threads are initialized with childregs->status = SR_PP | SR_PIE — setting Previous-Privilege to Supervisor/Machine and Previous-IE to 1. Same concern as start_thread: the full status register is assigned directly without any masking or sanitization of the remaining bits. If a child kernel thread's pt_regs structure contains stale data for MPRV, MXR, TVM, TW, TSR from a previous use on the stack (unlikely on kmalloc but structurally possible), those bits could propagate into the CSR_STATUS on context switch via __switch_to's restoration path.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:169-183  handle_exception
      On kernel entry from an exception, the code clears SR_SUM | SR_FS_VS (and SR_ELP on 64-bit) from CSR_STATUS via csrrc at line 183. This is a subset-clear: it only zeroes SUM (so kernel code can't accidentally access user memory) and FP/Vector status (to detect illegal FP/vector usage in kernel space). MPRV, MXR, TVM, TW, TSR are NEVER cleared or checked on exception entry. If any of these bits were set (by M-mode firmware, a buggy context-switch path, or hardware reset), the kernel executes with those bits active and undetected. For example, MPRV=1 on kernel entry would cause load/store instructions to use MPP's privilege mode for address translation — a silent violation of kernel memory access expectations.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:441-451  __switch_to
      Context switch saves CSR_STATUS to TASK_THREAD_SUM_RA(a3) and restores only the SR_SUM bit (masked via 'and s0, s0, s1' where s1=SR_SUM) into CSR_STATUS via csrs. This is a bitwise-OR operation: it sets SUM if it was saved, but never clears non-SUM bits that the outgoing thread may have set in the status register. Over time, bits like MPRV, MXR, or TVM could accumulate in CSR_STATUS if any code path or firmware sets them and the kernel never clears them — the context-switch path neither saves them for the outgoing thread nor sanitizes them for the incoming thread. The status register's non-SUM/FS/VS/ELP bits are effectively uncontrolled across context switches.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:130-133  secondary_start_sbi
      csrw CSR_IE, zero clears only the interrupt-enable CSR (MIE/SIE) and csrw CSR_IP, zero clears pending interrupts. No mstatus bits are explicitly initialized on secondary hart boot — MPRV, MXR, TVM, TW, TSR, SUM, endianness bits are left entirely at hardware reset value (or whatever M-mode firmware left them at). The kernel implicitly trusts that the M-mode firmware (OpenSBI) or hardware reset state sets these bits to a safe value. On hardware where mstatus bits hardwire to read-only 0, this trust could be misplaced with no detection.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:217-220  _start_kernel
      Same pattern as secondary_start_sbi: only IE and IP CSRs are zeroed on primary hart boot. No mstatus initialization. The primary boot path has the same implicit trust in M-mode firmware/hardware reset state for all mstatus bits beyond IE.
  [OK] arch/riscv/mm/fault.c:334-334  do_page_fault
      The SR_SUM check (!(regs->status & SR_SUM)) correctly detects kernel access to user addresses without uaccess wrappers — a well-established safety check. This is the only mstatus bit (beyond user_mode()'s SPP checking) that the page fault handler inspects. The code is correct for what it checks; the gap is that MPRV and MXR are never inspected, meaning a page fault caused by MPRV-based address translation or MXR-based execute-only page access would not be attributed to the mstatus state — the handler would see a normal page fault and potentially attribute it to the wrong cause.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/lib/uaccess.S:28-41  fallback_scalar_usercopy
      The assembly user-copy path manages SUM (li t6, SR_SUM; csrs CSR_STATUS, t6 ... csrc CSR_STATUS, t6) but never saves/restores or even inspects any other mstatus bits. If MPRV or MXR were set in CSR_STATUS before this function was called, they remain active during the user copy — the csrs/csrc operations only affect bit 18 (SUM). This is a structural issue: no kernel code path includes a full status-register save/restore around operations that modify mstatus bits, meaning bits modified by one code path can leak into unrelated kernel execution.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/suspend.c:15-42  suspend_save_csrs
