PR #2978  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Hardwiring mstatus/mstatush control bits to read-only despite the associated feature being present silently disables M-mode's ability to enforce privilege boundaries, trap lower-mode operations, and configure memory-access policy.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/riscvreg.h:82-123  (defines)
      MSTATUS bit definitions for MXR (bit 19), MBE (bit 37), SBE/UBE (bits 36/8 on RV64), TVM (bit 20), TW (bit 21), and TSR (bit 22) are absent. While the kernel runs in S-mode and cannot directly write mstatus, these missing definitions mean: (a) no M-mode firmware code or boot stub can reference these bits symbolically, (b) no KASSERT or boot-time check validates that the underlying hardware supports these writable bits, and (c) static analysis tools scanning for 'all standard RISC-V privilege-spec bits' would flag a false gap. Since MXR controls whether execute-only pages are readable by M-mode, and TVM/TW/TSR control M-mode trap delegation for virtual-memory/WFI/sret, their absence from the kernel's bit-definition header reflects an implicit assumption that M-mode firmware (OpenSBI) will configure these correctly — an assumption not validated anywhere in the FreeBSD tree.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/riscvreg.h:60-80  (defines)
      SSTATUS bit definitions cover SIE, SPIE, SPP, SUM, FS_MASK, XS_MASK, SD — the bits the kernel actively manipulates. However, MXR (bit 19) is absent from sstatus as well. The RISC-V privileged spec defines MXR as readable/writable in sstatus when S-mode is implemented. The kernel never sets or reads MXR via sstatus, meaning there is no execute-prevention enforcement analogous to SMEP (amd64) or PXN (arm64). This is confirmed by mitigations.7:354 which documents '-' for RISC-V execute-prevention. If a future hardware implementation hardwires MXR to read-only 0 in mstatus while supporting S-mode virtual memory, the kernel has no code path to detect or compensate. Currently safe only because no RISC-V hardware with MXR exists, but the semantic gap is explicit.
